# InterGenOS keyboard-shortcut defaults — the terminal key.
#
# WHY A dconf SYSTEM DB AND NOT A gschema OVERRIDE (the same structural
# argument as 00-intergenos-dock and 02-intergenos-shell-effects beside this
# file, for a different reason). System-wide GNOME defaults belong in
# /usr/share/glib-2.0/schemas/*.gschema.override, and show-desktop is set
# there, in config/gsettings/92_intergenos-desktop.gschema.override, because
# org.gnome.desktop.wm.keybindings has a fixed path.
#
# A custom keybinding does not. Its per-binding schema
# org.gnome.settings-daemon.plugins.media-keys.custom-keybinding is
# RELOCATABLE: it declares no path, and each binding lives at a path chosen at
# runtime. A gschema override can only set keys in a schema that has a fixed
# path, so it cannot carry one of these at all. The dconf layer is path-based
# and schema-independent, which makes a system db the only mechanism that can
# ship a default custom keybinding.
#
# THE LIST KEY IS HERE TOO, DELIBERATELY. custom-keybindings itself lives in a
# fixed-path schema and could sit in the gschema override, but the list and the
# binding it points at are one setting: a list entry with no section is a
# dangling path and a section nothing points at is never read. Splitting them
# across two mechanisms would give one setting two writers, which is the drift
# shape this tree has already had to close once.
#
# NOT LOCKED. A user who wants the key for something else changes it and their
# user db wins. This is a default, not policy.
#
# WHAT IS BOUND. Ctrl+Alt+T opens the terminal the image ships,
# /usr/bin/gnome-terminal, named by absolute path so what the key runs depends
# on the image rather than on the session's PATH. The path is the one
# packages/desktop/gnome-terminal declares in its own verify_paths, and the
# preflight gate tests/preflight/test_desktop_keybindings_are_shipped.py fails
# the build if the two ever stop agreeing.
#
# WHY IT IS BOUND AT ALL. GNOME ships no built-in terminal key, so on a stock
# desktop the combination does nothing. Measured on an installed R001.1 system
# on 2026-08-24: custom-keybindings was empty, while the user documentation and
# the first-run page both told the reader the key opened a terminal. Decided
# 2026-08-24: bind it rather than document its absence.

[org/gnome/settings-daemon/plugins/media-keys]
custom-keybindings=['/org/gnome/settings-daemon/plugins/media-keys/custom-keybindings/custom0/']

[org/gnome/settings-daemon/plugins/media-keys/custom-keybindings/custom0]
name='Terminal'
command='/usr/bin/gnome-terminal'
binding='<Control><Alt>t'
